# Declaring what a flat drop MEANS, then reducing it one axis at a time.
#
# Drop a folder of images on one palette row (hold ALT to make them a group),
# then right-click the group -> "Sort by filename" so the order is what you
# think it is. A drop gives a flat list; shape() says how to read it.
#
# The layout is row-major: the LAST axis named varies fastest. For
# shape(g, position=2, exposure=3) the three exposures of position 0 come
# first, then position 1's three -- which is the order a shoot is taken in.

frames = image("shoot")

# Six images = 2 camera positions x 3 exposures.
# Adjust these to match what you actually dropped; shape() checks the count.
shot = shape(frames, position=2, exposure=3)

# Reduce one axis at a time. merge_mean knows nothing about this: it takes N
# images and returns one, and the framework runs it once per remaining
# coordinate. [position=2, exposure=3] -> [position=2] -> a single image.
merged = merge_mean(shot, over="exposure")
final  = merge_mean(merged, over="position")

display(final, "merged")
